  "content": "Looking at the line\u2011angle (skeletal) drawing, this is a **cage\u2011type polycyclic hydrocarbon \u2013 adamantane** (tricyclo[3.3.1.1\u00b3,\u2077]decane, C\u2081\u2080H\u2081\u2086), i.e. the smallest \"diamondoid\": a carbon cage in which every six\u2011membered ring sits in a chair conformation (the perspective view of fused chair hexagons with the internal \"Y\" bridges is exactly how the adamantane cage is drawn).\n\nKey features supporting this:\n- Three parallel vertical C\u2013C bonds and the upper/lower \"Y\" (three\u2011fold) bridgeheads correspond to the four methine (CH) cage corners linked by six methylene (CH\u2082) bridges.\n- All rings are fused chair\u2011form cyclohexane hexagons (the zig\u2011zag hexagon outlines in the sketch), characteristic of the diamond\u2011lattice cage rather than a flat aromatic or simple monocyclic structure.\n- Bond/vertex count matches the C\u2081\u2080 cage: 12 C\u2013C bonds (4 CH + 6 CH\u2082 vertices).\n\nIdentity:\n- **Name:** adamantane (tricyclo[3.3.1.1\u00b3,\u2077]decane)\n- **Formula:** C\u2081\u2080H\u2081\u2086\n- **SMILES:** `C1C2CC3CC1CC(C2)C3`\n- **Class:** tricyclic cage (diamondoid) hydrocarbon; the fully chair\u2011conformed, strain\u2011free cage that makes it unusually stable.",
